WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
The Romance Languages

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.080
I guess this is the part where I make a cliché joke 
about the language of "romance."

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.080
Ok, Cut! Cliché joke over. Finished.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.620
Hello everyone! Welcome to the LangFocus channel, 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.100
Today I'm going to be talking 
about the Romance languages.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.340
Not the language of Romance, 
but the Romance languages.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.940
Why should you care about the Romance languages?

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.500
Well, the most important Romance languages are:
French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, and Romanian.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.440
They are some of the most widely
 spoken languages in Europe,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.640
and some of them are spoken
widely around the entire world.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.260
There are 800 million native speakers
 of Romance languages around the world.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.760
That's why you should care.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.420
They are a group of languages that belong to the 
Italic subfamily of the Indo-European language family.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.380
The Italic languages are languages
 originating in what is now Italy.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.620
What is the capital of Italy?

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.420
Yes, it's Rome.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.820
For answering that question correctly, you win...

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.420
absolutely nothing!

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:08.400
Sorry!

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.020
"Italy", "Italic"

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.180
"Rome", "Romance"

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.740
I see a connection.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.200
Which brings me to my a frequently asked question.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.240
Why are they called the "Romance Languages"?

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.220
They're called the Romance Languages 
because they all developed from Latin

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.560
which was the language of the Romans 
and the Roman Empire.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.620
As the Roman Empire spread
 throughout Western Europe,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.840
the local population in most 
of those areas adopted Latin.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.720
Two forms of Latin were used throughout the Empire.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.880
One of them was Classical Latin, which was 
the formal language used by the upper classes.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.020
And there was Vulgar Latin which was the common, 
everyday speech of the common-day people.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:50.860
Vulgar Latin is the Latin that spread amongst
the local populations of the Roman Empire.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.200
They called it "Vulgar Latin" 
because they used to say things like

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.640
[censored] you, mother[censored]!

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.080
Get [censored]!

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.600
Actually, that's not true. 
"Vulgar" just means "common or vernacular".

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:10.520
Over time, Vulgar Latin in the different areas 
of the Roman Empire evolved separately 
into different varieties of spoken Latin.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:17.540
And after the fall of the Roman Empire, 
they started to diverge even more, 
and eventually, they evolved into separate languages.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.300
Unlike today, in those days there was no TV, 
no radio, no universal education

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:29.180
to keep people anchored to the standard form of Latin. So the spoken forms of Latin diverged quite easily.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:35.140
It's hard to count the number of Romance languages 
in existence, because they form a dialect continuum.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.800
That means that if you draw a line from Portugal to Italy, 
or from Southern France to Northern France,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.780
any two villages along the way 
would be able to understand each other.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.940
But if you move further along that line,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.120
it becomes more difficult for them to communicate because their dialects become more divergent.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.320
So it was difficult to determine where one language ended and the other began.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.500
But, when modern nation-states arose,
 and free education was introduced,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.720
certain local dialects were chosen 
to become standard, national languages

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.460
such as Parisian French in France
and Castilian Spanish in Spain.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.960
This has caused many of the old dialects 
to collapse into a smaller number 
of national languages and regional languages.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:21.060
The main Romance languages of today are :
* Spanish with 410 million native speakers

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.960
* Portuguese with 216 million native speakers
* French with 75 million native speakers

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.960
* Italian with 60 million native speakers and
* Romanian with about 25 million native speakers

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.300
I should probably also mention Catalan,
which has 4 million native speakers.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.000
Catalan is a language spoken 
in the Catalonia region of Spain.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:47.520
42% of the EU's population (or 210 million people)
are Romance Language speakers.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.680
That's only 26% of the total number 
of Romance language speakers.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.360
So... where are the others?

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.280
As European countries like France, Spain
 and Portugal colonized the world,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.960
they spread their languages to countries
 all over the globe.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.140
Today, Spanish is spoken by 377 million 
people in the Western Hemisphere,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.060
where it is the official language of 19 countries.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.820
Spanish is not the only language spoken
 in the Western Hemisphere.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.640
Portuguese is spoken by 202.6 million in Brazil.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:24.360
That makes Portuguese the majority language in South America,
even though it's only spoken in that one country.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.480
Portuguese is also spoken in 5 African countries.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.640
It's an official language in Macau,
but it doesn't seem to be used much anymore.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:35.820
And some people still use it it in East-Timor
where it used to be the official language.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.600
Actually, Portuguese is now once again
one of East-Timor's official languages.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.780
As, for French, it is spoken as a native language 
by 7.7 million people in Canada,

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.560
10 million people in the Carabbean

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.720
It is also widely spoken as a second language
and a lingua franca in Africa,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.540
where it is spoken by 115 million people
in 31 different countries.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:00.480
Inside Europe, it is not only spoken in France,
but also inside Belgium and Switzerland.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.300
And Luxembourg and Monaco.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:07.220
Unlike Spain, France and Portugal,
Italy started building colonies quite late in the game.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:10.280
So they didn't really established 
their language  abroad very much.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:16.640
It was spoken in Lybia and Somalia, but now, it seems 
to be only spoken by a handful of elderly people there.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.980
Within Europe though, Italy is spoken 
as a native language in Italy, and also San Marino,

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:23.500
The Vatican and Monaco.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:31.160
And it's also widely spoken as a second language in some 
nearby countries, like Croatia, Slovenia, Albania and Malta.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.220
Standard Italian is based on the dialect of Tuscane,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.460
but there are numerous other varieties of Romance 
speech in Italy, some of which can be considered 
their own languages.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.860
One example of that is Sardinian, which is spoken 
on the island of Sardinia, by approximately 1 million people.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.060
But, these days, the younger generation doesn't seem 
to speak Sardinian very much.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.240
Instead, they learn a mixture of Sardinian and Standard Italian
which has kind of become a regional dialect of Standard Italian.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.780
Another language that has declined
 somewhat  in this way is Occitan,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:08.000
which is spoken over an area covering part of northern Italy,
southern France and a little bit of Spain.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.820
Like Sardinian, Occitan is mainly used
 amongst the older generation,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:15.420
and while young people can understand it,
they don't really speak it that much.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.960
Occitan forms a dialect continuum 
with the Catalan language in Spain.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.660
That means that a Catalan speaker and an Occitan speaker
from nearby regions can understand each other,

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.160
even though they are speaking different languages.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.360
Unlike Occitan which is fading, Catalan is  a thriving 
regional language in the Spanish region of Catalonia.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.500
It has 4 million native speakers and
5 million second language speakers there.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:45.220
There are several other regional languages that are co-official 
with Castilian Spanish in the respective regions in Spain.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:51.800
These regional languages have remained much stronger 
amongst the younger generations, 
than the regional languages of France and Italy have.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.900
Are the Romance Languages mutually intelligible?

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:00.300
Well, I want all the speakers of Romance Languages 
to comment on that down below and share their experiences.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.440
Can you understand people when they speak
other Romance Languages? Let us know.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:07.620
But generally, it seems that the answer is yes and no.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.340
Sometimes, in certain cases, they can understand 
each other but only to a certain extent.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.460
And in other cases, 
communication is quite straight-forward.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.760
From what I understand, the hignest degree 
of mutual intelligibility is between Spanish and Portuguese,

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.600
or between Spanish and Italian,
or between Italian and Romanian.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.980
But sometimes, that intelligibilty 
goes mostly in one direction.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.440
For example, Portuguese speakers can often
understand Spanish people very well.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.180
But Spanish speakers can't understand the Portuguese 
speakers because of the complex phonology.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:43.060
Will learning one Romance Language 
help you learn another one? The answer is YES!

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:47.300
It won't make it automatic or super easy,
but it will help, of course, because...

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:51.860
all of them come from the same Latin roots.
And much of the vocabulary comes from Latin.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.720
And much of the grammatical structure
is similar and is based in Latin.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:01.680
So, if you love learning languages and you want a language 
that you can use in lots of places around the world,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.140
then one of the major Romance Languages 
would be a great choice.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:09.380
So get on it. Learn a Romance language.
A language of love.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.360
Thank you for watching the Langfocus Channel.
Be sure to leave your comments down below.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.500
Especially if you speak a Romance language.
Tell us your experiences.

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.520
Alright. Have a good day.

